# Complete NVMe I/O on the CPU that submitted it.
#
# rq_affinity=1, the kernel default, completes a request anywhere in the
# submitting CPU's cache group. rq_affinity=2 completes it on the submitting
# CPU itself, so the completion path touches a request structure that is
# already hot in that core's L1/L2 instead of pulling it across the group.
#
# The reason this is worth a rule HERE rather than a line in a generic tuning
# list: the target is an 8 P-core + 16 E-core Arrow Lake-HX with no SMT, where
# "the submitter's cache group" spans cores of two different microarchitectures
# at two different clocks. A completion that lands on an E-core for I/O a
# P-core issued is the case this closes, and it is a case a homogeneous CPU
# does not have.
#
# This composes with user.slice's io.latency target rather than duplicating it
# (see systemd/system/user.slice.d/10-pulsar-io-latency.conf): that drop-in
# decides WHOSE I/O wins under contention, this one decides where the
# completion interrupt is serviced. Different layers, no overlap.
#
# DEVTYPE=="disk" excludes partitions, which have no queue/ directory at all --
# without it every nvme0n1p* event logs a failed attribute write on every boot.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="disk", KERNEL=="nvme*", ATTR{queue/rq_affinity}="2"
